8.1 The lay of the land
=======================

subtle plays an audio or video file in mpv (https://mpv.io) and shows
its subtitles in Emacs as ordinary read-only text.  You hear (and see)
the media in mpv; you read in Emacs.  The cue being spoken is
highlighted, and the text follows the audio.

   It is made for studying a language from films, podcasts and lectures,
so a few things are done on purpose:

   • The text stays still until the page needs turning.  You can look at
     a word without it running away.
   • If you scroll by hand, the autoscroll pauses by itself and a small
     notice tells you how to bring it back.
   • You can open two subtitle files at once, side by side: the language
     you study and one you know.
   • Layouts such as paragraphs turn cue fragments into running text.

   You need Emacs 29.1 or later and mpv on your ‘PATH’.


File: subtle.info,  Node: Your first session,  Next: The keys,  Prev: The lay of the land,  Up: Tutorial

8.2 Your first session
======================

  1. Run ‘M-x subtle-open’.  Pick a media file, then one or two subtitle
     files (‘.srt’ or ‘.vtt’).
  2. Or do it from Dired: put point on the media file (or mark the media
     and its subtitles) and run ‘M-x subtle-dired-open’.  Subtitles
     named like the media file (‘film.mkv’ and ‘film.sl.srt’) are found
     for you.
  3. mpv starts and the subtitles appear.  Read along.
  4. Press ‘?’ for a box listing every key.  Press ‘q’ to quit; it asks
     before it closes anything.


File: subtle.info,  Node: The keys,  Next: Trying options live,  Prev: Your first session,  Up: Tutorial

8.3 The keys
============

All keys are single letters, so they also work in a modal editor's
motion state.  The ones you will use every minute:

Key           What it does
-----------------------------------------------------
‘p’           pause or resume
‘b’ and ‘f’   skip back and forward
‘(’ and ‘)’   previous and next cue
‘RET’         play from the cue at point
‘{’ and ‘}’   slower and faster
‘=’           back to normal speed
‘r’           make the text follow the audio again
‘?’           show the help box
‘q’           quit

   The rarer commands sit behind a prefix key: swap the two windows with
‘s s’, choose the layout with ‘s v’, change any option with ‘s o’, and
toggle automatic resume with ‘s t’.

